On Sat, 20 Oct 2007 05:12:55 +0200, Colin Watson  
<cjwatson@chiark.greenend.org.uk> wrote:

>> The question is, should I implement TTY output in UTF-8 encoding as a
>> compile-time option, or as a new windowing port based on the regular TTY
>> port, so that it's selectable at run-time?
>
> The encoding should absolutely be detected from the locale at run-time;
> many systems have users using a variety of encodings and there's really
> no good reason for any program to have the encoding hardwired in at
> compile time. Without knowing much about the relevant parts of Nethack,
> but having a reasonable amount of experience with writing encoding-aware
> code, I think encoding detection should be done across the board if the
> necessary library support is available, perhaps with an option to
> disable it. I'm not sure I see why this would be a new port rather than
> just fixing the existing one.

That's the kind of answer I wanted: "best practices" from someone who's  
been there. Thanks!

>   http://www.cl.cam.ac.uk/~mgk25/unicode.html#activate

Ah, nice FAQ, with snippets. Thanks again!

